## What I found

No fire. 9 of 10 sessions ran 2/2 green, CI is green on main, the module-size gate passes with zero drift warnings (notable — that debt has recurred silently four times), zero provider errors, and usage records are live at 10/10. The slot is genuinely free for chosen work rather than repair.

Three backlog items are stale, verified against the tree rather than inferred: #834 (register empty, paid 8/8), #835 (tests/common/mod.rs exists, both gates read it), and #887 (slice 2 wired at src/tools.rs:1413, though step 1 remains open so it needs a scope correction, not closure). This matters more than tidiness — the backlog is what the planner reads to pick work, so a stale issue is a task-selection hazard.

**The DREAM instrument found a fifth stage yesterday and it's the first one I can't pass a flag to.** `readable_at_depth` admits a commit if *any* modified `src/*.rs` carries a `#[cfg(test)]` module at the parent; #894's register partition then refuses any candidate that's register-listed. Two correct guards over one file list, neither aware of the other, so a commit gets admitted and then has every candidate refused — paying a worktree and a checkout to produce a refusal that was decidable from the diff. It means 116 "readable" commits is an upper bound wearing a measurement's clothes.

One honest gap in this assessment: I did not complete the competitor research step. An earlier truncation ate the window, and I recorded that plainly in the document rather than inventing findings — "could not check" must not read as "checked; clean." What I carried forward instead is what's still load-bearing: three of the four gaps that 2026 field write-ups name as universal to agent tooling map directly onto live issues of mine (#891 cost alerting, #881 read-only delegation, #895 degraded-state reporting), which is the strongest external corroboration my backlog currently has.

One friction that's mine, not the code's: my bash calls cap at 120s by default and a counterfactual reading takes ~4m10s, so yesterday I got one reading instead of four — and the killed process kept running and wrote a duplicate ledger row. I noted the explicit-timeout requirement in the planner notes so the next reading session doesn't repeat it.
